    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The seven-day search found only the stable-fingerprint requests. It cannot rule out misuse before that window or through some other leak. It also does not establish that the burst itself was misuse.\n- **A2: Cannot tell.** The check scored the account after the burst. We are not shown whether the burst's data had reached the check by then.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link between the accounts. It does not prove they have one owner.\n- **A4: No.** Having no report is not the same as the payouts being legitimate. A victim may not have noticed, and an incident finding is only recorded if someone investigates.\n- **A5: Yes.** Several things point the same way: a new fingerprint through a residential proxy 19 minutes after publication, then a payout redirect, a new webhook and faster payouts in one burst.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: Yes.** D has 0 chargebacks in 100 transactions. A, B and C each have 2 in 100.\n- **B2: No.** The 5 percent figure combines all four accounts and includes alerts. Alerts per account are not shown, so one account could be above 5 percent.\n- **B3: No.** The search found no other accounts, but that does not show none exist. Accounts held under nominees or name variants could be missed.\n- **B4: No.** These are two analysts' opinions, probably based on the same evidence. Writing on different days does not make them independent confirmations.\n- **B5: No.** The payment was blocked, so it never completed and could not draw a chargeback. Having no chargeback says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6: Yes.** Eleven storefronts share templates and a descriptor pattern and settle through A to D. That is enough to justify investigating them together.\n\nRESULT MO-083 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Cannot tell | A3: No, not by itself | A4: No | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: No | B4: No | B5: No | B6: Yes"
